Inka provides one block, `slate`; the admin no longer registers `listing`. A frontend declares every other block it uses, a listing included (`title`, `group`, `blockSchema`, and its item type and mapping recipe: see Listings). A declared block with no `blockSchema` now stops the editor loading, and a block type in content that the frontend doesn't declare is an error in the content check, not a warning. A block that is a window onto a page field (title, description, lead image) sends `disableCustomSidebarEditForm: false`; Inka no longer recognises them by name. @djay
